course: dictionary
lesson: 10244
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one you need. Toll is a good example. «Drivers had to pay a toll to cross the bridge» is about money. «The death toll rose after the flood» is about the number of people killed or hurt.
explain: Toll can also be an action word. «The bell began to toll for the funeral» means a large bell rang slowly, again and again. «Years of hard work began to take their toll» means the hard work slowly caused tiredness and harm.
explain: Use the clues around the word. Ask: is this about money, a number, or a sound? Then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: «Toll» means money you must pay to use a road, bridge or tunnel.
fact: «The death toll» means the number of people killed or hurt.
fact: «To take a toll (on)» means to cause damage, harm or tiredness over time.
fact: «To toll» means a large bell rings slowly again and again, often for a death.
fact: From 1429 to 1857 Denmark charged a toll, called the Sound Dues, on every foreign ship passing through the Øresund strait.
exercise: «Drivers had to pay a toll to cross the bridge.» What is a toll here? | The number of people hurt | A phone number you can call for free | *Money you must pay to use the bridge | A large bell ringing slowly
exercise: «The death toll rose after the flood.» What does toll mean here? | Money paid at a gate | A bell ringing slowly | *The number of people killed or hurt | A phone call that costs nothing
exercise: «The bell began to toll for the funeral.» What does toll mean? | To pay money to cross | To add up the costs | *To ring slowly again and again, often for a death | To make a phone call free
test: «Years of hard work began to take their toll on her.» What does take a toll mean here? | To pay money to someone | To count the people who were hurt | To ring a bell slowly | *To cause tiredness and harm over time
test: The shop has a toll-free number. What does toll-free mean? | *A number you can call without paying | A number that rings slowly | A number that costs more than usual | A number for counting the death toll
exam: «Guard bees check each incoming bee, though a stranger bringing nectar is sometimes let in, as if she paid a toll.» What does toll mean here? | A phone call that costs nothing | *A price or fee paid for being let in | The number of bees counted at the door | A large bell ringing slowly
